-- Example: Annotations in L4
-- Demonstrates @desc, @nlg, and @ref annotations

§ `Example: Annotations in L4`
    GIVEN age IS A NUMBER @desc "Person's age in years"
          applicantAge IS A NUMBER @nlg "the applicant's age"
          eligible IS A BOOLEAN @ref "Section 4(1)(a) of the Act"

#EVAL age

-- A literal percent sign or close bracket in an `@nlg` annotation is written
-- with a backslash in front of it. Here the rate range `10%and%20` would
-- otherwise read `and` as a parameter name, silently.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the levy on` amount [a rate between 10\%and\%20 applied to %amount%]
MEANS amount TIMES 0.15

#EVAL `the levy on` 100

-- An opening `[` never needs escaping, but escaping it is accepted, because
-- an author who escapes the closing bracket of a citation escapes the opening
-- one in the same keystroke. Both spellings mean a literal bracket, and
-- `l4 nlg` prints this one as "see note [3] on `amount`" with no backslash.
--
-- The annotation sits immediately after the name it describes. Put it after
-- the parameter instead and it annotates the PARAMETER, which is why the
-- phrasing would not show up at the call site.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the surcharge on` [see note \[3\] on %amount%] amount
  IS amount TIMES 0.05

#EVAL `the surcharge on` 100

-- A citation containing a literal `>>` needs a backslash before the angle
-- bracket, or the inline `@ref` form would end halfway through it.
GIVEN claimant IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is eligible` claimant MEANS
  <<Section 4(1)(a) \>> as amended>>
  claimant

#EVAL `is eligible` TRUE

-- A heralded `@nlg` may carry a language subtag. The tag is recorded and
-- round-trips; nothing selects on it yet, and an untagged annotation is
-- unchanged by its existence.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a large amount` @nlg:en the amount %amount% is large
  IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `is a large amount` 150
